tests/relocatable-design: what the `-r` design (WP-154) measured
===============================================================

This is reference material, not tests. Nothing here is run by `cargo test`,
by `cargo xtask linktest` (which reads only `*.test` files under tests/link)
or by the gate. The record `spec/landed/layout/WP-154.md` and the M4-r rules
in spec/ cite these paths relative to this directory (`exp/tools/rcheck.py`,
`work/final.log`, ...).

The scripts are copied unchanged from the design's scratch tree, so they
still name the absolute paths they were run from:
- `/Volumes/AgentWork/mortar-helper/.build/target-wp-154/...`;
- the mortar binary `mortar-3ad9867a`, a debug build of main at 3ad9867a;
- kiln++ in `.build/target-kiln-next/release`, and hod in
  `.build/target-hod/release`;
- the BusyBox tarball in `.build/real-builds/downloads`;
- the cxx-large-g build tree of `.build/bench-work`, read-only.

To re-run, copy `exp/` into a scratch tree, point those paths at it, and run
`exp/run-all.sh WORK`. The review timed it at about 3.5 minutes on the
aarch64 host, one link at a time, plus about 2 minutes for prelink and
bench (the first version said 25 minutes). BusyBox builds with make -j4.
`exp/run-all.sh WORK review` also runs the fix pass's scripts (below),
which add a few minutes; gap.sh writes two outputs of about 570 MB in
all and deletes them.

Tools: GCC/G++/gfortran 14.2.0 (Debian 14.2.0-19, native and cross
x86_64/i686), clang 19.1.7 and 22.1.8, GNU ld 2.44 (and binutils 2.44
objcopy), ld.lld 22.1.8, mold 2.37.1, qemu-user 10.0.13, glibc 2.41, gdb 16,
lldb 19, Python 3.13. Host: AArch64, 4 KiB pages.

Layout
------

exp/run-all.sh WORK  every area below, in order, one log per area.
exp/tools/relf.py    a small ELF reader for ELF32 and ELF64 (sections, groups,
                     symbols, relocations; REL addends are read from the bytes
                     by the i386 width table). It shares nothing with mortar.
exp/tools/rcheck.py  OUT IN...: the prototype of the structural rules that the
                     record's verifier requests ask for (V-R2..V-R6). It places
                     each input section in the output by masked bytes, then
                     checks every input relocation: once, with the same type
                     and an equivalent target (name and addend for globals,
                     output section and offset for local and section targets,
                     the same string for merged strings). Its known limit: it
                     places a rewritten `.eh_frame` by bytes, so GNU ld's and
                     mold's `.eh_frame` show "unexplained" records. A
                     verifier must parse CIEs and FDEs (V-R5).
exp/tools/secs.py    FILE [REGEX]: prints name(type,flags,align,size).
exp/census/          A: the ld line that each driver writes for -r and its
                     variants (gcc/g++/gfortran native and cross, clang-19/22
                     for three targets). The lines are in
                     work/census-lines.txt (399 lines).
exp/busybox/         A: BusyBox 1.36.1's -r lines (Kbuild) captured from a
                     real build. applets/built-in.o is redone by each
                     reference and by mortar, the final trylink line is
                     replayed with GNU ld and with mortar, and the program
                     runs.
exp/corpus/          the C (c1-c4, mainc) and C++ (x1, x2, mainx) components
                     of item D.
exp/final/           D: every target x compiler x component x {static, PIE},
                     -r by each reference, the final link by GNU ld and by
                     mortar, run and compared with the direct link. The log
                     is work/final.log; the record's table summarises it.
exp/facts/run.sh     B: the mini-cases of items 1-8, one line per reference
                     (work/facts.log). Its row 4-addrsig-comment is wrong: its
                     inputs define a symbol twice. supp.sh replaces that row
                     with 4b and adds CREL, GOTPCRELX, nested -r, EI_OSABI and
                     build-id (work/facts-supp.log).
exp/options/         A/B: about 60 options with -r and the input cases
                     (archives, groups, a DSO, /dev/null, -b binary), per
                     reference (work/options.log).
exp/moldbug/         mold 2.37.1 -r: COMMON becomes SHN_ABS, and the IFUNC
                     crash.
exp/debug/           B.5: -O0 -g C++, with gdb and lldb at x1.cc:10 and
                     x2.cc:8 and backtraces, comparing each route with the
                     direct link (work/debug.log).
exp/none-overlap/    D finding 1: GNU ld -r's R_*_NONE at the r_offset of a
                     live .eh_frame record, which mortar refuses on AArch64.
exp/meta/            B.8: kiln++ units through each reference's -r, with
                     --odr=strict on the direct route and through -r; a legal
                     pair; hod objects (work/meta.log).
exp/more/run.sh      the pre-link idiom, -gsplit-dwarf, Fortran, -i, LTO
                     objects, --gdb-index and -T with -r (work/more.log).
exp/more/prelink.sh  the pre-link idiom with C++, with EH and gdb: plain -r
                     against --force-group-allocation, then objcopy
                     --localize-hidden (work/prelink.log).
exp/bench/           E: time and peak RSS of each reference's ld -r over 400
                     objects of cxx-large-g, and the output's tables
                     (work/bench.log).
exp/review/          the fix pass after the adversarial review (record §H):
                     rcheck-cxx.sh FINALWORK (work/rcheck-cxx.log, from
                     final/'s work directory), zkw.sh + secdiff.py (-z
                     keywords), stack.sh (.note.GNU-stack), unique-buildid.sh
                     (--unique, --build-id styles), equiv.sh (V-R1
                     candidates), gap.sh (the 77 MB gap by section),
                     anchors.sh + anchors.py (MT-16 anchors, Q10), fuse.sh
                     (-fuse-ld lines), mapsym.sh (AArch64 $d). Each takes
                     WORK and writes work/<name>.log's content to stdout.
                     work/review-x.log is the review's own x02-x09 rerun
                     from its read-only directory; those scripts are not
                     copied here.
